WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:03.543
(audience cheering)

00:00:04.956 --> 00:00:06.277
- It's nice to meet you.

00:00:06.277 --> 00:00:07.517
- [Austin] Nice to meet you.

00:00:07.517 --> 00:00:08.550
- You are incredibly talented.

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:09.383
- Oh, thank you.

00:00:09.383 --> 00:00:11.130
- This film, people are gonna freak out.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.290
I'm just saying, you're incredibly gifted.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:16.470
So you're playing such an icon, Elvis.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:18.900
Were you musical as a kid?

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.780
- My dad gave me a guitar
when I was 13 years old

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.610
and I obsessed about it.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.470
I would play for eight hours a day.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.661
My fingers would bleed and
I'd Super Glue 'em shut

00:00:27.661 --> 00:00:32.220
and keep playing, but I
never played for anybody.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.590
It was like therapy.
- You were shy about it?

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:35.820
- [Austin] So shy.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.300
- Oh my gosh, you were shy and
you ended up playing Elvis?

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.520
That's ironic.
(audience laughing)

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.470
Did you play anything else?

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:44.940
- Yeah, I played the piano as well.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.340
I picked that up about
a year after the guitar.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:50.130
And that opened up, you learn a lot

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.290
about music playing the piano.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.480
It opened up a lot.
- I love that.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.850
And it's like, even if you
didn't become a musician

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.850
in a band, it's an amazing
thing, I think, even my kids

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:00.780
I'm like, "You don't have to be in a band,

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.070
but I want you to play something."

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.824
I think it's good for you.
I think it's therapeutic.

00:01:04.824 --> 00:01:05.657
- Do they play instruments?

00:01:05.657 --> 00:01:07.050
- My daughter plays violin a little bit.

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:09.570
My son is six. He hasn't started yet.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.160
- Violin was the first
thing I learned how to play.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.860
- Me too. It was violin and then piano.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.930
- Were you shy as a kid?

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:19.096
- Yes, until music. Interesting.

00:01:19.096 --> 00:01:20.196
- That broke you out.

00:01:20.196 --> 00:01:21.594
Yeah, nobody believes me.
(audience laughing)

00:01:21.594 --> 00:01:23.520
But I was shy at some point in my life.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:24.480
My mom will tell you.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.500
And I was very, I don't
know, just introverted,

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.830
and then music brought me to life,

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:33.060
my personality and everything.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.440
I think anytime when you're a kid,

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.960
I don't know if you agree,
you find that one thing

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.660
that really helps you flourish
and find your personality

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.910
and find out who you are.
- That was acting for me.

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:44.100
- But you started acting
in a weird way, right?

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.990
Was it your brother?
(Austin laughing)

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.240
- Yeah, I had a stepbrother
and he got scouted

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.490
at the Orange County Fair.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.163
And we all thought, "Oh,
he's gonna be a movie star.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:56.610
He's gonna go off and
do all these things."

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:58.890
And so I just tagged along as he went up

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:01.050
to this audition in Los Angeles.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:04.410
And when they saw that
my mom had another kid

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:07.140
they were like, "Does he wanna do it too?"

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:08.854
My mom said, "Are you interested?"

00:02:08.854 --> 00:02:10.770
I don't know what came over me that day,

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.350
'cause I was so shy, but I said "yeah"

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:17.430
and then it turned out it was
a background talent agent.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:18.870
So it got you into doing extra work

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.420
on Nickelodeon shows and stuff.

00:02:21.672 --> 00:02:22.505
And that's what got me into it.

00:02:22.505 --> 00:02:24.653
- That's the door.
Everybody needs the door.

00:02:24.653 --> 00:02:25.992
- When I got my first manager

00:02:25.992 --> 00:02:27.416
and started going to acting class,

00:02:27.416 --> 00:02:29.370
and acting class broke me
out of my shyness, in a way.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:31.260
That gave me an outlet for it.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.870
- Did they make you just
do the most awkward things?

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.270
- Yeah, always.
- Okay. I've never done it.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.370
I wanna do it just for fun,

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:41.255
just to force myself to
hate my life a little bit.

00:02:41.255 --> 00:02:42.715
(Austin and audience laughing)

00:02:42.715 --> 00:02:43.830
You're supposed to do
things that you don't think

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.683
you'd be good at to grow or whatever.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.899
It feels like it would terrify me.

00:02:49.899 --> 00:02:50.976
(audience cheering)

00:02:50.976 --> 00:02:53.130
That was the look at him as
Elvis in the new movie "Elvis".

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:54.660
The movie is amazing.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:57.423
So can you explain to
everybody how you got the role?

00:02:58.260 --> 00:02:59.760
- I sent Baz a tape.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.980
I heard that he was making
the film and I sent him a tape

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.770
of me singing "Unchained
Melody" and playing the piano.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:05.853
- God, I love that song, classic.

00:03:05.853 --> 00:03:07.856
- Such a beautiful song.

00:03:07.856 --> 00:03:11.220
And he responded to it and
then I flew to New York

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.553
and we met and we just
talked for three hours.

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:17.970
And then he said, "You
wanna come in tomorrow

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.539
and read a couple scenes from the script?"

00:03:20.539 --> 00:03:22.736
And so I came in and read a
couple scenes and then he said,

00:03:22.736 --> 00:03:26.280
"Do you wanna come in tomorrow
and sing a couple songs?

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:27.870
And you wanna sing 'Suspicious Minds'?"

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:30.120
And so I went home that night
and practiced and I come back

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.250
we ended up doing that for five months.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:33.637
- What?

00:03:33.637 --> 00:03:34.650
- It was unlike any audition,

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.300
where it was just five
months of exploring.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:40.608
- That's a relationship.
(audience laughing)

00:03:40.608 --> 00:03:43.701
- And then it came time
for the screen test,

00:03:43.701 --> 00:03:45.941
- [Kelly] Then it came time for the, okay.

00:03:45.941 --> 00:03:49.141
- There comes a point where
the studio has to sign off.

00:03:49.141 --> 00:03:52.059
And you gotta do the screen test.

00:03:52.059 --> 00:03:55.560
And so I'd prepared everything
and got to the screen test

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.143
and Baz changed everything
on me on the day.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.710
I was supposed to do
a couple scenes and he

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:05.280
changed the scenes and changed
all the words in the scenes.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.040
And then I was supposed
to sing three songs.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.137
And then he starts describing
the scene to me and he saying,

00:04:11.137 --> 00:04:12.630
"You're gonna sing these three songs."

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.760
And they weren't the
songs that I'd prepared.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.867
And so suddenly I'm on set and I go,

00:04:16.867 --> 00:04:19.317
"Baz, I don't think I have the
material that you gave me."

00:04:19.317 --> 00:04:20.700
And he goes, "Oh no, we changed it,

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.280
but I'll get you new material."

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.680
And then turns out he
never got me new material

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:28.740
and he starts filming while
I'm learning everything.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.750
So then I start going through it

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.437
and then he cut one of
the scenes and he said,

00:04:32.437 --> 00:04:34.980
"Now we're gonna do you
performing 'Suspicious Minds'."

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.760
- Is he just messing
with you at this point?

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:38.763
(audience laughing)

00:04:38.763 --> 00:04:41.301
I would look around for
Ashton Kutcher at this point.

00:04:41.301 --> 00:04:43.879
(Austin laughing)
Am I being punked?

00:04:43.879 --> 00:04:44.970
That's horrible.

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:46.710
- He put me through the wringer.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:50.583
And I realize now he likes to
be very spontaneous on set.

00:04:51.821 --> 00:04:53.250
He wanted to see if I would lose my mind.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.230
And so I didn't think I got it.

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.030
And then a week later I got a call

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.310
and I woke up to Baz calling me.

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:00.960
And I thought, "Okay this is the moment,

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.101
I either have it or I don't."

00:05:03.101 --> 00:05:05.850
And he's very dramatic in the
way that he goes about things.

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:08.280
He sounded downcast. He goes--

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.116
- [Kelly] Oh, how mean. (laughs)

00:05:10.116 --> 00:05:12.150
- "I just wanted to be the
first one to call you and say,

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.640
are you ready to fly, Mr. Presley?"

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.861
It's just the greatest
phone call I've ever had.

00:05:16.861 --> 00:05:17.694
(audience applauding)
- I love that.

00:05:18.781 --> 00:05:22.140
But that's a lot of
emotional roller coaster,

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:23.730
up and down to get there.

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:25.983
Do you think that it
might have been because,

00:05:25.983 --> 00:05:30.030
I heard Denzel called him because
y'all had worked together,

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:31.980
and was trying to help you get the role

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:33.870
'cause he loved you and believed in you.

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:35.557
Do you think it's something
he said that was like,

00:05:35.557 --> 00:05:38.418
"Nah, Imma see how much he
really will work"? (laughs)

00:05:38.418 --> 00:05:40.838
- I don't know if that played
into it. It's possible.

00:05:40.838 --> 00:05:42.960
- I don't know that anyone
has that in this business.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:46.350
Oh, Denzel called Baz.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:49.080
Did he know Baz?

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:50.813
- [Austin] No, they never met before.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.303
- Denzel Washington calls you.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.810
I know, look at your teary eyes.

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:00.573
- He's powerful. He's
been my hero for so long.

00:06:02.623 --> 00:06:03.727
I went into that play going,

00:06:03.727 --> 00:06:05.400
"I'm not gonna try to be Denzel's friend."

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:06.930
I'd read Ethan Hawke talking about them

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:08.460
doing "Training Day" together

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:09.810
and he talked about this as well.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:11.647
And so I went in there and I thought,

00:06:11.647 --> 00:06:13.080
"I'm just gonna do my job."

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:15.390
And so I showed up the
first day of rehearsal

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:19.230
I'd memorized a four hour
play, such an intense play.

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:22.050
And we did it and at a certain point

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:24.660
Denzel started seeing
that I was showing up

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.490
earlier than he was to get to the theater.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:28.719
And so he'd show up a little bit earlier.

00:06:28.719 --> 00:06:29.722
(Kelly laughing)

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:30.555
And then I'd show up a little bit earlier.

00:06:31.853 --> 00:06:33.570
Eventually one day it was
just he and I and he thought

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:35.490
that he was there before
me and I came down,

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.678
I'd already been up in my dressing room.

00:06:37.678 --> 00:06:39.510
And it is just the dark theater

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:41.580
and he's sitting on the stage by himself

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.103
and I came walking down, he goes,

00:06:43.103 --> 00:06:44.730
"I thought I'd beat you here."

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:46.946
And I said, "No, I've
been here for an hour."

00:06:46.946 --> 00:06:47.779
(Kelly laughing)

00:06:47.779 --> 00:06:48.826
- But nice try.

00:06:48.826 --> 00:06:49.659
- He goes, "I got an idea for you."

00:06:49.659 --> 00:06:51.060
And he took me under his wing

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:52.860
and started giving me acting advice.

00:06:55.261 --> 00:06:56.843
And that was really huge for me.

00:06:57.690 --> 00:06:59.580
He didn't tell me he was
gonna call Baz at all.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:02.340
He'd never met Baz.
- That's so nice.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:03.173
- Very sweet.

00:07:03.173 --> 00:07:05.610
- I love that you made
such an impact on him

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:07.319
that he did something like that.

00:07:07.319 --> 00:07:09.660
- Very kind.
- That's very, very cool.

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:12.900
You did get to meet Priscilla Presley.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.390
What was that like? Was it intimidating?

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:19.260
- Super intimidating. But she
didn't make it intimidating.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:20.520
I met her the same day

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.290
I went to Graceland for the first time.

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:23.894
- I've never been.

00:07:23.894 --> 00:07:25.196
- [Austin] You've never been?

00:07:25.196 --> 00:07:26.130
- No. And I lived in Tennessee for years.

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:27.543
I just don't know, work.

00:07:29.011 --> 00:07:30.513
- It's amazing. It's so great.

00:07:30.513 --> 00:07:34.240
Baz and I took this road trip
right after I got the part

00:07:35.332 --> 00:07:37.292
where we went and recorded in Nashville.

00:07:37.292 --> 00:07:39.630
It was the first recording
studio I was ever in.

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:41.415
- It's a great place to record.

00:07:41.415 --> 00:07:45.210
- Studio B there. Where Dolly
Parton recorded "Jolene."

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:48.693
Elvis recorded over 270 songs, I believe.

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.790
So we took this road trip
and ended up in Graceland.

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:57.210
And next door, there's this
hotel where I met Priscilla.

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:01.830
And she was just so, so beautiful
and so warm and welcoming.

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:03.637
And she just gave me a big hug and said,

00:08:03.637 --> 00:08:05.726
"You have a lot of support."

00:08:05.726 --> 00:08:08.823
I walked outta that hotel room
just with tears in my eyes.

00:08:10.634 --> 00:08:13.920
It's amazing when you look into her eyes

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:17.220
and you think, "This is the same woman

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:19.860
that Elvis was madly in love with.

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:21.810
She was madly in love with him.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:24.150
She's the mother of his only child.

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:25.770
And then she's right here in front of me."

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:27.423
It's really surreal.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.842
We got to go to the Met Gallery recently

00:08:30.842 --> 00:08:33.870
and we danced a bit
and, and she's the best.

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:35.490
She's incredible.
- I love that.

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:37.020
You seem very present.

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:40.740
Are you always that present
and thinking like that in life?

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:41.760
Just being in the moment like that?

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:43.440
Not many people are like that.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:46.110
- I try to be. It's hard
with what we do sometimes.

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:49.080
- I'm working at it and I just
turned 40, Austin. (laughs)

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:50.130
- Well, you look amazing.

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:52.286
- Well, thank you for lying. (laughs)

00:08:52.286 --> 00:08:53.340
(audience laughing)

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:55.737
But thank you so much, Austin.

00:08:55.737 --> 00:08:56.570
Everybody, we need another break.

00:08:56.570 --> 00:08:58.740
Go see "Elvis," it's in theaters tomorrow.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:02.757
You won't regret it.
(audience cheering)

